## Units

| # | Unit and compelling question | Days | Skills | Owned standards | Practices and concept areas | What students learn | Why this unit, here | Check set adds | Sensitive topics and teacher notes |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | How a message travels. *When I type a note, save it and send it to a friend, what parts does it pass through, and what keeps it safe?* Anchor: a note typed on one classroom device turns up on another across the room, and the class maps where it went | 5 | g5-hardware-software; g5-network-parts; g5-cia-triad | E5-SYS-HW-12; E5-SYS-NT-14; E5-SYS-SE-13 | Concept area: systems and security. Practices: IC3 (explain how the parts work together); CT6 (break a task into input, processing, storage and output); CT7 (the pattern shared by wired and wireless networks); HCD12 (availability: users can reach their work when they need it); ESR2 (keeping a password and personal information private) | Session 1 (unplugged, then device): students act the parts of a computer (input, processor, memory and storage, output) while a key press becomes a letter on a screen, then trace typing a note and saving it through the four-part system-diagram, saying which part is hardware, which software, and where the note is kept when the device is off; a sensor (a classroom thermometer's reading) is named as another input. Session 2 (unplugged): the parts of a wired and a wireless network labeled on a network-diagram (device, cable, switch, router, access point, modem) and each one's job; the class acts the parts with yarn and traces the note from session 1 to another device, then sorts the parts into wired, wireless or both. Session 3 (unplugged): the cia-triangle, one story a side: a locked diary (only the right people see it), a note changed on the way or a photo that may not be real (it stays correct), a library closed when you need a book (it is there when needed); students match new threats to the side they hurt and say why that side matters, and keep a pretend password private on a paper account. Out: no logic gates, binary or circuits, no protocols, IP addresses or packets (grade 6's g6-packets), no difference between local networks and the internet, no encryption or access control lists, no real account or real school network examined. End-of-unit (day 4): label a new system-diagram and trace a new task through it (E5-SYS-HW-12), sort new network part cards and give each one's job (E5-SYS-NT-14), match four new threats to the triangle's sides with a reason (E5-SYS-SE-13); respond day 5. Tools: any school device with a plain note tool (job: the note typed and saved, session 1). Curio Class writes: role cards for the acted computer and network, the system-diagram and network-diagram mats, the triangle's stories and threat cards, the paper account | First because every later device session stores, sends or protects something: the four-part system-diagram says where unit 3's variables and unit 4's data live, and the triangle names unit 4's data risks. Networks sit before the triangle because the triangle's threats happen on the network-diagram (model_in of g5-cia-triad). Grade 4's three systems rows are fresh, so three sessions meet three ids. Base: archived Year 6 "Communication and collaboration" lesson 6 for session 3's privacy side; sessions 1 and 2 our own | Candidate: kept, the partial base's "communicating responsibly" order (what to share, then why) inside session 3, zero added days | Passwords and personal information: no real password, address or name is said or written; the teacher page carries health's "If a student tells you something" steps word for word. No student is asked what devices or networks are at home. Yarn lies on the floor, students walk. Devices carried with two hands, cords behind the table. Agent reads: sensitivity. |
| 2 | Flowcharts for a game. *How can we draw a game's rules so anyone can follow them, and who is the game for?* Anchor: a step counter's rules (when a step is sensed, add one; when the count reaches the goal, cheer) drawn as a flowchart the class traces together | 5 | g5-visual-algorithm; g5-human-centered | E5-ALG-PS-01; E5-ALG-IM-03 | Concept area: algorithms and design. Practices: CT6 (say what the game must do); CT7 (a repeat and a variable box reused in each flowchart); CT8 (plan the flowchart before any code); IC3 (a partner traces your flowchart and says what it did); HCD10 (include a user the first design left out); ESR1 (whose values shaped a design, and who it was unfair to) | Session 1 (trace, then modify): a given flowchart for the step counter starts on an event, repeats a step, holds a variable box (count) that changes each time round and a decision diamond that reads it; students trace it on a trace-table, then change the goal in the decision and trace again. Grade 4's numbered sentences become boxes, diamonds and arrows. Session 2 (make): pairs plan, then draw a flowchart for a score game of their own (start event, repeat, score variable, decision on the score) on index cards with branches or in formal symbols, trace it on a trace-table and check it against a partner's trace; every control structure appears, across one or more drawings. Session 3 (society and design): human-centered design in four ideas, ask who will use it, include different users, test with them, improve, and the considerations a designer weighs (user needs, accessibility, fairness, the designer's own values); students find each idea, or its absence, in a familiar given technology (a library catalog search page), say how people made a choice there that was unfair or wrong for some users, fill a benefit-risk-chart, and change one design of their own session 2 game for a player it leaves out. Out: no lists or arrays, no procedures, no more than one level of nested repeat or decision, formal flowchart symbols a help not a demand, no technical detail of how the given technology works, no code yet (unit 3). End-of-unit (day 9): draw a flowchart for a new task with an event, a repeat, a decision and a variable and trace it (E5-ALG-PS-01); for a new given technology, name two human-centered ideas it uses or misses and one change for a user it leaves out, with the reason (E5-ALG-IM-03); respond day 10. Tools: none (all unplugged). Curio Class writes: the step-counter flowchart and its trace-table, flowchart index-card sets, the library-catalog design case with screenshots we draw ourselves, the benefit-risk-chart | After unit 1 because the flowchart's variable is a named box in the system-diagram's memory. Before unit 3 because the flowchart is unit 3's plan: session 2's score games become the programs unit 3 builds and its team project assembles, so a design is planned and checked for its users before code (the bar's design before build). Base: archived Year 6 "Variables in games" lesson 4 (designing a game) for session 2, and "Sensing movement" lesson 5 (designing a step counter) for session 1's anchor; session 3 our own | Candidate: kept, the partial base's design-sheet order (player, rules, score, end) on session 2, zero added days | Session 3 has disability content (a user who cannot see the screen or use a mouse): talk is about what a design lets a person do, never about a classmate, in the wording the sensitivity read settles from its pack. The step counter is about a toy or a game, never a student's own body, weight or fitness. Agent reads: sensitivity (the starting pack in Sensitivity reads first). |
| 3 | Programs that keep score. *How does a program remember the score, and how do we find what went wrong when it does not?* Anchor: the class's score game from unit 2, built in blocks, that adds a point but never says "you win" | 7 | g5-variables-use; g5-systematic-debug; g5-remix-program; g5-team-project | E5-PRO-VD-06; E5-PRO-TR-08; E5-PRO-PD-04; E5-PRO-PD-05; E5-PRO-RD-07 | Concept area: programming. Practices: CT7 (reuse a component); CT8 (plan the build from the flowchart); CT9 (test, debug and refine); HCD11 (test the game with a player who did not write it); IC4 (roles, tasks and a project note); IC5 (driver and navigator switch at the halfway signal); ESR2 (credit the code you reuse) | Session 1 (trace, unplugged first, then device): students act as the computer with labeled paper cups as named boxes for a four-line program, then trace programs that store a value, compare it and change it (a score that goes up, a countdown timer that triggers an event at zero, a typed guess compared with a pretend password); students trace each on a trace-table before it runs, then run it step by step; "set score to score plus 1" is not equality. Session 2 (modify): change the compare value, the update or the start value of a working score game and predict, then check, what changes. Session 3 (debugging as a method): given buggy programs tried with three systematic strategies in turn (trace by hand, test one part at a time, take pieces out to isolate the bug), plus two more the boundary names, reading the tool's message and adding a temporary output (a sound or a shown value) where the bug may be; a test-table records each; students say which strategy found the bug and why. Session 4 (modify): new buggy programs, each pair chooses a strategy, fixes the bug and writes the line "what I tested and what I changed". Session 5 (make, remix): a worked remix is traced first, then pairs modify a working game and combine it with a part of another (a teacher or peer program) to make a new variation (a level, a timer, a new ending), crediting the source in a comment. Session 6 (make, team): teams of three, roles named in advance, build the score game from a unit 2 flowchart: each student builds one component (the score with a variable and a decision, the movement, the timer) and writes an inline comment and a short project note another student could use to put it in place. Session 7 (make, team): components assembled and tested by a student who did not write them, with a test-table; bugs fixed by the session 3 strategies; the team's project note finished (purpose, parts, credits). A rubric with three look-fors gives each team feedback, not a score. Out: no lists, arrays or variable scope, no formula with more than one variable, no version control, no professional debugging tools or automated tests, no generated code, ten lines a program at most. End-of-unit: the mid-year course-level check (day 18) stands for it. Tools: our own block editor (blocks with a text view and a step-through trace; job: trace, modify and build the programs; tool ask for TOOL-ASKS.md). Curio Class writes: every worked, buggy and remix program with its text view and tested solution, the trace-tables and test-tables, the paper-cup named-box set, the component templates and project-note sheet, the team rubric | After unit 2 because the programs build its flowcharts and its variable box, and after unit 1 because a variable lives in the system-diagram's memory. Trace before modify before make on each construct: variables (sessions 1, 2), debugging (3, 4), remix and team make (5 to 7). The team project comes last because it uses all four rows. Placed across weeks 11 to 17, which include the core check weeks 11, 16 and 17, because it holds no course-level check. Base: archived Year 6 "Variables in games" lessons 1, 2, 3, 5 and 6 (Source map) | Candidate: kept, the partial base's improve-and-share step folded into session 7's test by a non-author, zero added days | Pair and team roles are named and switch, so no student sits out or holds the keys all session; a team member with a motor or vision disability takes the navigator or tester role by choice, and the editor works by keyboard alone. The pretend password program uses a made-up password, never a real one. Credit is taught as respect for another person's work. Devices: two hands, cords behind the table. Agent reads: sensitivity. |
| 4 | Data that trains a model. *Can we teach a computer to tell our shapes apart, and what should we worry about when tools learn from data?* Anchor: a model trained on the class's first drawings calls every small triangle a circle | 5 | g5-collect-tools; g5-variability; g5-train-model; g5-data-benefits-risks | E5-DAT-DC-09; E5-DAT-DI-10; E5-ALG-ML-02; E5-DAT-IM-11 | Concept area: data and analysis, with machine learning (algorithms and design). Practices: CT8 (plan the training set, then train); CT9 (test the model on new examples and improve the set); CT7 (patterns and outliers in a dataset); IC3 (explain the variability); ESR1 (who gains and who is at risk); ESR2 (protect the people a dataset is about) | Session 1 (device): students collect data of two kinds with a tool, a pretend class survey on a form (which of four indoor games, how many paper-airplane loops) and the classroom thermometer's readings, then organize them in a data-table, label each column numeric or descriptive, sort a numeric column from highest to lowest and group the descriptive answers in the tool. Session 2 (unplugged and device): a given dataset of repeated toy-car ramp times (invented for practice) and the class's own three trials: values are not all the same; students mark the typical values and the outlier on a dot chart, give possible sources (starting the timer late, the car bumping the side, a recording slip, a real change in the ramp) and name one relationship between two columns (ramp height and time). Session 3 (device, make): pairs build a labeled training-set of shapes drawn on the tool's drawing pad (circles, triangles, stars), train a small classifier and test it on new drawings kept in a separate test set, counting how many of ten it got right. Session 4 (device, modify): which drawings are missing from the training-set (small, tilted, thin-lined shapes), add more varied examples, retrain and compare the counts; the class says why training data changes what a model gets right. Session 5 (society): case cards of everyday technologies that use collected data (a weather forecast, a route planner, a step-count tracker, a recommendation list); students fill a benefit-risk-chart (convenience, better predictions; privacy, wrong results, bias), name the cia-triangle side at risk, as review, and give one limit that would make it safer. Out: no mean, median or other statistic computed (math grade 6), no percents (accuracy is a count out of ten), no math of how training works, no making a data tool from scratch, no chart required of the collected data (E5-DAT-DC-09), no technical detail of how a case technology works. End-of-unit: the year-end course-level check (day 25) stands for it. Tools: a form and table tool (job: collect and organize, sort and group); a model-training tool with a drawing pad that keeps everything on the device (job: train and test; tool ask for TOOL-ASKS.md). Curio Class writes: the pretend survey, the ramp dataset (invented for practice), the dot chart, the drawing prompts, the pretend-model card set for the paper path, the four case cards with dated source lines | After unit 3 because the table and the model are read with the trace and test habits of unit 3 (the test-table becomes the model's test counts). Collecting and spreading data comes before training because a training-set is data collected and organized; the benefits and risks come last because students have just made a model that was wrong for some shapes. Base: archived Year 6 "Introduction to spreadsheets" lessons 1 and 2 for session 1; sessions 2 to 5 our own | Candidate: kept, the partial base's collecting-then-formatting order on session 1, zero added days | Class data is about things, never people: no prompt asks about a student's home, body, food, pets or family; the survey is pretend and names no one. No camera or microphone: the model learns from drawings, never faces or voices. Case cards name no company. Ramp: a book-and-board slope on the floor, cars are not thrown. Agent reads: sensitivity. |
| 5 | Who computing includes, and who decides. *Who were computing tools built for, and when should a person, not a computer, decide?* Anchor: a sign-up form on screen that will not accept a classmate's real-sounding invented name because it has a space and an accent mark | 6 | g5-who-included; g5-use-or-not; g5-professionals | E5-SOC-HI-16; E5-SYS-IM-15; E5-SOC-ET-17; E5-SOC-HU-18; E5-SOC-CE-19 | Concept area: computing and society (with systems and security, how systems change culture and work). Practices: ESR1 (who gains, who is left out); HCD10 (design with the people who use a tool); HCD12 (people kept informed and in control); CT6 (does this problem need computing at all?); IC3 (two views, one dated source each) | Session 1: cases where leaving a group out shaped a tool (a form that does not fit every name, a voice tool that does not hear every accent) and cases where including a group shaped it (captions made with Deaf users, a keyboard for a language made with its speakers, an early programming language written in English-like words so people who were not mathematicians could program), told by what happened with no company named, two views and a dated source each (STANDARD.md "Real cases in society days"); students place them on the timeline and list who was included or left out, what it changed, and one change they would make. Session 2: how computing systems change culture and the ways people live and work, helping and hindering (how sharing short videos changed how families keep in touch and how news spreads; how a job such as a translator's or an illustrator's is changing as generative tools arrive, from a dated labor source), without students using those tools. Session 3: when to use computing, from case cards (counting votes in a class election, translating a short sign, choosing who gets the last turn, comforting a friend), with a benefit-risk-chart and one question, should a person decide this?; then why a family might choose to use or not use a new home device (cost, privacy, who in the family can use it), without saying whether a choice is right. Session 4: how professionals in different fields work together with shared digital tools (a wildfire team, firefighters, weather scientists and mapmakers on one live map; a museum exhibit team, an artist, a historian and a programmer on one shared plan), then a short role-play of a class tool's team (designer, programmer, tester, user): what each adds and what goes wrong when one is missing. Out: no independent historical research, no dates or names beyond a major milestone's own, no ethical frameworks, laws or error rates, no use of social media or generative tools, no judgment of a family's choice. End-of-unit (day 31): for a new case, say who was included or left out and what it changed (E5-SOC-HI-16); one way a new system helps and one way it hinders how people live or work (E5-SYS-IM-15); two things a new family weighs about a new device (E5-SOC-ET-17); decide and justify whether a new problem needs computing (E5-SOC-HU-18); how two new professionals share their work with a digital tool (E5-SOC-CE-19); respond day 32 | Last because it gathers the year: unit 2's design for users, unit 3's teams and unit 4's model that was wrong for some shapes become the evidence for who a tool includes and who should decide. Its rows need nothing taught after them and hand on only to grade 6, so it can follow the year-end check. Base: archived Year 6 "Communication and collaboration" lessons 3 to 5 for sessions 2 and 4; sessions 1 and 3 our own | Candidate: dropped, an interview with a school worker who uses shared digital tools; case cards carry the id and a visit needs the school's own arrangement, zero days | Session 1 has disability, language and naming content: names on cards are invented, no student's own name is tested on the form, and the language keyboard case is replaced if its community's own account cannot be found (Sensitivity reads). Session 2 touches work and automation: some families' jobs are changing, so no student is asked about a family member's job, and a student's own offer is welcome. Session 3's family case is a pictured family, never a student's own home. Agent reads: sensitivity (the starting pack in Sensitivity reads first). |

## Course-level checks

| Check | After unit | Check days | Respond days | What it checks |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Course-level check, mid-year (week 18; it stands for unit 3's end-of-unit assessment) | 3 | 1 | 1 | Day 18 scores unit 3 in full with unseen items and samples units 1 and 2. Unit 3, written: trace a new score program on a trace-table and predict its output (E5-PRO-VD-06); on a new buggy program, find and fix the bug and name the strategy used, with a test-table line (E5-PRO-TR-08); on the device or its paper version, add a given credited part to a given working program to make a new variation, with the credit comment (E5-PRO-PD-04), and build a missing timer component for a given team program with its inline comment and the note a teammate needs to put it in place (E5-PRO-PD-05, E5-PRO-RD-07), recorded as separate look-fors. Units 1 and 2 sampled with three new items: trace a new short flowchart (E5-ALG-PS-01), match a new threat to the cia-triangle (E5-SYS-SE-13), name the part a new task's data is stored in on the system-diagram (E5-SYS-HW-12); unit 2's end-of-unit record for E5-ALG-IM-03 is carried unscored. Written paper of about 25 minutes and a build task of about 10. Day 19, the respond day for unit 3 and the check, reteaches by error letter and gathers fresh evidence on the missed items |
| Course-level check, year-end (week 25; it stands for unit 4's end-of-unit assessment) | 4 | 1 | 1 | Day 25 scores unit 4 in full with unseen items and samples units 1 to 3. Unit 4: in the table tool, label a new table's columns numeric or descriptive, sort one and group the other (E5-DAT-DC-09; part met on the paper path); on a new dataset, mark the typical values and the outlier and give two possible sources of the difference (E5-DAT-DI-10); for a given model that misses a stated kind of drawing, choose and add training examples, retrain and report the new count out of ten (E5-ALG-ML-02; part met on the paper path, where the student chooses the examples and predicts); for a new case card, a benefit-risk-chart with one limit that makes it safer (E5-DAT-IM-11). Units 1 to 3 sampled with three short new items: a wired or wireless part and its job (E5-SYS-NT-14), the value of a variable after a short new program (E5-PRO-VD-06), the first wrong line in a new buggy program (E5-PRO-TR-08); unit 3's team project record is carried unscored. Unit 5 follows in weeks 27 to 32 and is checked by its own end-of-unit assessment. Day 26, the respond day for unit 4 and the check, reteaches and records each student's support and gains for grade 6 |

## Cross-subject

Both leans are to core courses of grade 5 in the same year, and both core units come later in the year than the
computer science session that uses them, so each session teaches what it needs itself. Math and science grade 5 meet
every school day; this course's day n falls in week n.

### Mathematics, grade 5

| Row | Lean | Used here | Earliest math unit that teaches it | Bridge if math teaches it later |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| g5-visual-algorithm | m5-two-patterns (5.OA.B.3: two numerical patterns from two rules, in a table) | Unit 2, sessions 1 and 2 (weeks 6 and 7) | math grade 5 unit 10 (days 135 to 149, weeks 27 to 30) | Math comes later. The flowchart's counter adds the same amount each time round (by 1, by 2, by 5), counting on that grades 2 and 3 math have taught; the trace-table lists the counter's value each time round, and no second pattern, ordered pair or graph is asked, so the session stands without the math unit |

The year-end check and unit 4 use counts out of ten and whole-number comparison only; no mean (math grade 6) and no
percent (math grade 6) is used (lesson 13).

### Science, grade 5

| Row | Lean | Used here | Earliest science unit that teaches it | Bridge if science teaches it later |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| g5-variability | s5-design-test (3-5-ETS1-3: fair tests with several trials) | Unit 4, session 2 (week 21) | science grade 5 unit 6 (days 100 to 117, weeks 25 to 30) | Science comes later. The session teaches what it needs itself: why the same trial run several times gives different times, using a given dataset and three class trials, and names the sources of difference; it does not plan a fair test or change a variable, which science unit 6 does, and science can then reuse the ramp dataset idea as its own retrieval |

No row leans on English language arts or social studies. Writing a project note, explaining a strategy and weighing
two views of a case are practiced here, not owned.

## For the lead

1. **After-core check weeks (calendar pass).** This course's checks sit in weeks 18 and 25, off the grade 5 core
   check weeks 10, 11, 16, 17, 21, 22 and 29 to 32. docs/courses/CALENDAR.md shows grade 5 week 18 already holding the
   mid-year checks of dance, media arts, theater, visual arts, physical education and ELD, and week 25 media arts' year-end
   check. They stay for the lead's one calendar pass, under the pick in the kindergarten frame's
   For the lead 1. If the pass needs week 18 freed, week 19 or 20 works by moving one unit 3 session ahead of the
   check, and the frame says which; weeks 12 to 15 would need the check after unit 2 and a new unit split.
2. **Year 6 of the partial base.** The pilot maps Year 1 to kindergarten, so Year 6 is mapped here to grade 5. The
   Year 6 lessons were placed from their titles and unit overviews on the archived pages (2026-11-25), not from the
   lesson files; the placement is checked when the store folder is made (pilot For the lead 4). Offered on:
   "Communication and collaboration" lessons 1 and 2 (internet addresses, data packets) to the grade 6 frame
   (g6-packets, MS-SYS-NT-34). The "Sensing movement" unit needs a microcontroller board (STANDARD.md section 5,
   optional); only its step-counter design idea is used, on paper.
3. **The block editor's needs for grade 5 (tool asks).** Our own editor starts with grades 3 to 5 (STANDARD.md "Our
   own editor"). Grade 5 needs from it: variables with set, change and compare blocks; an event block that fires when a
   value is reached; a temporary "show value" or sound block for debugging; comments shown in the text view; and a way
   to paste one saved program's blocks into another (the remix and the team assembly), all in one file with no
   account. A separate small model-training tool with a drawing pad and on-device training is also asked (unit 4);
   until it exists, E5-ALG-ML-02 is met only on the paper path, marked part met. Whether Curio Class builds a training
   tool is a scope call (Cory's); my pick is yes, small and on-device, since grades 4, 5 and 6 rows all lean on one.
4. **Source list.** docs/APPROACH-SOURCES.md, beside the pilot's entries: the archived key stage 2 Year 6 unit pages
   (raspberrypi.org/curriculum/archived/key-stage-2), the National Association of the Deaf's captioning pages, the
   W3C Internationalization article "Personal names around the world", and the US Bureau of Labor Statistics
   Occupational Outlook Handbook (bls.gov/ooh/) as the dated labor source for unit 5 session 2.
5. **Row changes** (notes and words cells; the lead applies them, listed in the final message): g5-professionals
   gains the boundary's cross-field professionals sharing work through digital tools; g5-variability drops "average"
   for "typical" and says no statistic is computed; g5-collect-tools names numeric and descriptive columns and the
   sort and group in the tool; g5-train-model draws on a drawing pad (no camera or microphone) and adds the look at
   what is missing from the training data; g5-human-centered adds the designer's values and accessibility and
   fairness considerations, and its "human-centred" becomes "human-centered"; g5-variability's "analysed" becomes
   "analyzed".

## Source map

The partial base for grade 5 is the archived edition of England's Teach Computing Curriculum, Year 6 (six units;
raspberrypi.org/curriculum/archived/key-stage-2, resources dated 2023). Placed from the public unit pages (lesson
titles and unit overviews), opened 2026-11-25; each archived Year 6 unit lists six lessons. The publisher teaches each
unit's lessons in number order; where this frame splits a unit or takes part of it, the row says why. Each adapted
lesson keeps its idea, is rewritten in our words for an American grade 5, replaces the named block language,
spreadsheet and board with our own editor, a table tool and paper, and is credited once a day. Check days are ours,
never a kept lesson.

| Base unit (Year 6) | Lessons | Placed in | How it is used | Left out, and why |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Computing systems and networks: Communication and collaboration | 3 Working together; 4 Shared working; 5 How we communicate; 6 Communicating responsibly | Unit 5, sessions 2 and 4; unit 1, session 3 | Lessons 3 and 4: people working together through shared digital tools (unit 5 session 4); lesson 5: how computing changed the ways people communicate (unit 5 session 2); lesson 6: what to keep private, as the confidentiality side (unit 1 session 3), moved ahead because the triangle is unit 1's | None of 3 to 6 |
| Computing systems and networks: Communication and collaboration | 1 Internet addresses; 2 Data packets | None | | Both left out: addresses and packets are past the E5-SYS-NT-14 boundary and are grade 6's g6-packets (MS-SYS-NT-34); offered to the grade 6 frame |
| Programming A: Variables in games | 1 Introducing variables; 2 Variables in programming; 3 Improving a game; 5 Design to code; 6 Improving and sharing | Unit 3, sessions 1, 2, 5, 6 and 7 | Lessons 1 and 2: variables set and changed, traced first (sessions 1 and 2); lesson 3: improving a given game, as the remix (session 5); lessons 5 and 6: the designed game built and shared, as the team project's components, assembly and test (sessions 6 and 7). Sessions 3 and 4 (debugging strategies) are our own | None left out of these five; the named block language is replaced by our editor |
| Programming A: Variables in games | 4 Designing a game | Unit 2, session 2 | The game design sheet becomes the flowchart students draw, moved ahead of the code because the flowchart is unit 3's plan | None |
| Data and information: Introduction to spreadsheets | 1 Collecting data; 2 Formatting a spreadsheet | Unit 4, session 1 | Collecting data in a tool and organizing it by type in a table, sorted and grouped | None of 1 and 2 |
| Data and information: Introduction to spreadsheets | 3 What's the formula?; 4 Calculate and duplicate; 5 Event planning; 6 Presenting data | None | | All four left out: formulas, cost planning and charts go past E5-DAT-DC-09's boundary (no analysis or visualization asked) and use money and math this course does not teach; outside bar C1's 10 percent if kept |
| Programming B: Sensing movement | 5 Designing a step counter | Unit 2, session 1 | The step counter's rules as the anchor flowchart, on paper | Lessons 1 to 4 and 6 (the board, its inputs and making the counter): need a microcontroller board, optional in STANDARD.md section 5; left out, with the board named for a later optional unit |
| Creating media: Web page creation | Lessons 1 to 6 | None | | All six left out: no E5 id at this depth, depends on a web-page tool |
| Creating media: 3D modelling | Lessons 1 to 6 | None | | All six left out: no CSTA id, depends on a modeling app |

Units 1 (sessions 1 and 2), 2 (session 3), 3 (sessions 3 and 4), 4 (sessions 2 to 5) and 5 (sessions 1 and 3) are
our own: the partial base has no four-part system role-play, no network-parts unit at this age, no confidentiality,
integrity and availability, no human-centered design, no systematic debugging lesson, no variability, no machine
learning and no inclusion, emerging-technology or use-or-not cases.
# Course frame: Computer Science, Grade 4

Course `computer-science-grade-4` (catalogue id `cs-k12`), grade 4, one year. Draft frame, 2026-11-25, written on the shape of the kindergarten pilot (docs/courses/computer-science-kindergarten/frame.md and its fix report); the scope and sequence sets the days later.

## The course in brief

- **Units.** 5, one for each concept area at its grade 4 depth, in the order the ideas build: when it breaks and how
  it connects (systems and security, so the troubleshooting steps exist before the year's first device day that
  depends on them), algorithms in words (algorithms and design: a written algorithm with an event, a repeat and a
  choice, and who two algorithms help), tables, charts and people (data: a table in a computer tool, a chart that
  reports an investigation, and data about people), the class quiz, built in pieces (programming: trace, compare,
  debug as you build, then a team program with comments), and machines that learn and people who make tools
  (machine learning beside computing and society, the year's closer).
- **Days.** 32 course days: 28 in units, 2 for course-level checks (1 after unit 2, 1 after unit 4) and 2 respond
  days (one after each check). One lesson a week of about 40 minutes, 32 weeks: the computer science default for
  grades 3 to 5 (docs/courses/computer-science/STANDARD.md section 2, Cory, Desk q-fix-cs-1), a specials rotation,
  so course day n falls in week n. The 40 minutes hold set-up, clean-up and collecting work: a session opens with a
  three-minute retrieval trace (APPROACH.md, Pacing, "How a day opens"), and device sessions keep five minutes at
  the end to save or print work, shut lids and return devices, so a session teaches for about 32 minutes. Each unit's
  days include its end-of-unit assessment and end with a respond day (APPROACH.md, Pacing), with the pilot's one shape
  for a unit that a course-level check follows directly: in units 2 and 4 the check stands for the unit's own
  end-of-unit assessment and the check's respond day is the unit's respond day (pilot For the lead 2). So units 1 to 5
  teach on 4, 4, 4, 5 and 5 days, 22 of the 32. Days follow the load of the rows, not equal shares: unit 4 owns four
  programming rows and five ids, three of them device days with a trace, modify and make order and a team product with
  a feedback session before its score, and gets 5 teaching days; unit 5 owns three rows and five ids, two of them the
  rows the progression read counts as "two sessions in practice" (g4-ml-learning, g4-history-limits;
  docs/reviews/progression-computer-science-2026-11-19.md), and gets 5; unit 1's four systems rows are single
  unplugged or station sessions and get 4; unit 2's two rows get 4, three of them for the written algorithm (trace,
  modify, make); unit 3's three rows get 4, two for the investigation and its written report. 32 sessions for 16 rows
  is the read's "about two sessions a row", counted with checks. Each course-level check takes one 40-minute check
  session and one respond session. No unit runs over 25 days, so there are no mid-unit assessments.
- **Check weeks.** The grade 4 core checks take weeks 8, 9 (math's alternative), 10, 12, 16, 19, 21, 23, 27, 30, 31
  and 32 (docs/courses/CALENDAR.md, target weeks: ELA 16 and 32, math 12 (or 9), 23 and 31, science 10, 19 and 30,
  social studies 8 (or 12), 21 and 27). This course's checks sit in week 11 (mid-year, after unit 2) and week 24
  (year-end, after unit 4), with their respond days in weeks 12 and 25 (respond days are not check days). Week 24
  holds the year-end check so unit 5 has the seven weeks 26 to 32 its five ids need, as kindergarten's unit 5 keeps
  weeks after its year-end check; the weeks free of core checks after 24 (25, 26, 28, 29) are too few to fit unit 5
  before a later check. Units 3 and 4 run across weeks 13 to 23, which include the core check weeks 16, 19, 21 and
  23, because they hold no course-level check; unit 5's own end-of-unit assessment falls in week 31, a unit
  assessment, not a course-level check. Overlaps with other after-core subjects' check weeks are the lead's calendar
  pass (For the lead 1).
- **Standard.** The 19 E4 ids of the 2026 CSTA PK-12 Computer Science Standards, all taught here (STANDARD.md
  section 2): E4-ALG-PS-01, E4-ALG-ML-02, E4-ALG-IM-03, E4-PRO-PD-04, E4-PRO-PD-05, E4-PRO-VD-06, E4-PRO-RD-07,
  E4-PRO-TR-08, E4-DAT-DC-09, E4-DAT-DI-10, E4-DAT-IM-11, E4-SYS-HW-12, E4-SYS-SE-13, E4-SYS-NT-14, E4-SYS-IM-15,
  E4-SOC-HI-16, E4-SOC-ET-17, E4-SOC-HU-18, E4-SOC-CE-19. Each is taught at the depth its owner row's notes set, in
  our own words (STANDARD.md "Depth": the rows were checked against the boundary statements on 2026-11-21, which are
  never copied or quoted), and "What students learn" says what is out. Grade 4 writes an algorithm in words; the
  flowchart is grade 5's (STANDARD.md "Depth"). Plans cite ids and state each action in a short phrase of our own
  (STANDARD.md "Quoting"); the credit line "Standards: 2026 CSTA PK-12 Computer Science Standards (CC BY-NC-SA 4.0)"
  goes on any day that leans on a statement. Check set: each unit plan checks itself against the computer science bar
  (docs/ADOPTION-BAR-SUBJECTS.md part 5, items 1 to 9) and the lens (docs/agents/lenses/computer-science.md), and
  reads the partial base unit it adapts for order; the "Check set adds" column names what the writer expects that to
  add. Values rule (DESK-ANSWERS 2026-11-12) holds. No Specialty id, and no X + CS id, is claimed.
- **Practices and concept areas.** The Units table's fifth column (the C3 column of the core frames) names, for
  computer science, the concept area each unit teaches and the CSTA practices it teaches inside the content, by code
  and our label (STANDARD.md section 3), as bar items 1 and 2 ask. Each unit plan names the practice's day and task.
  Across the year: CT6 define the problem (units 1, 2, 3), CT7 find patterns and make reusable parts (units 2, 4, 5),
  CT8 build and plan (units 2, 4), CT9 test, debug and refine (units 1, 4), IC3 explain and give feedback (units 2 to
  5), IC4 run a project with shared goals and documented work (unit 4), IC5 be a reliable teammate (unit 4, driver
  and navigator), HCD10 learn from and include different users (units 2, 5), ESR1 weigh who gains and who is harmed
  (units 1, 2, 3, 5), ESR2 respect people and protect information (units 1, 3, 4: credit in a comment). Dispositions
  are named in the unit plans, not here.
- **Base text.** Partial: England's open computing curriculum, the Teach Computing Curriculum, archived edition
  (raspberrypi.org/curriculum/archived/key-stage-2, resources dated 2023), the same fixed edition the kindergarten
  frame maps (its For the lead 4). Year 5 is the grade 4 age (Year 1 is kindergarten in the pilot, Year 3 grade 2).
  License as the pilot states: the Open Government Licence v3.0 "unless expressly stated otherwise" on the publisher's
  terms page; the archived unit pages carry no license line of their own, so each file's license line is read when the
  store folder is made. Adapted unit by unit and rephrased for an American audience (STANDARD.md section 2, Cory
  2026-11-16): US spelling, school years named as US grades, no money in pounds, no product or company name, our own
  browser editor and table tool or paper in place of the named block language, database program and microcontroller.
  Three of its six Year 5 units feed units 1, 3 and 4 (see Source map); its two creating-media units and its
  physical-computing unit are left out, and the web-search half of "Systems and searching" is left to the library
  course's method. Units 2 and 5 are our own. The source's name appears only in a day's credit line, once a day.
  Pending(docs/sources/BASE-TEXTS.md#teach-computing-curriculum): the source store holds no copy of this base, so the
  Source map below rests on the public unit pages (lesson titles and unit overviews, opened 2026-11-25), and each
  adapted lesson's check for third-party images, text and tool screenshots waits on the store folder (For the lead 2).
- **Day kinds.** As docs/GLOSSARY.md (row "teaching day") defines them, in computer science's words (APPROACH.md):
  Understanding is an unplugged or trace session, the first teaching of a construct or idea (a written choose-your-path
  algorithm followed before anyone writes one, a counter program traced in a trace-table before it runs, a network
  acted out). Fluency is a modify session: change one line of a working algorithm or one part of a working program in
  small, checked steps; grade 4 has some of it (unit 2 session 2, unit 4 session 3, and the retrieval openings).
  Application is a make, design or society session: write one's own algorithm and have a partner follow it (unit 2
  session 3), run a data investigation and write its report (unit 3 sessions 2 and 3), build the team program (unit 4
  sessions 4 and 5), or judge cases with a chart (unit 2 session 4, unit 3 session 4, unit 5 sessions 3 to 5). Check is
  the end-of-unit assessment or a course-level check; Respond acts on it by error letter. Inside a unit: understanding
  before modify before make, and no make day before a trace or modify day on the same construct.
- **Evidence.** Grade 4 writes its answers (APPROACH.md, "The form of daily evidence"): trace and predict sessions end
  in an exit ticket with a short program or algorithm and "what happens?", wrong answers sorted by error letter;
  modify and make sessions are read from the program or algorithm itself against two or three look-fors, with one line
  from the student on what they tested and what they changed; society and data-impact sessions end in a short claim on
  the chart. Every end-of-unit assessment is a sitting with unseen items, never the unit's own anchor, about 30
  minutes, with a paper version: read and trace items plus a short build, debug or audit task, each with look-fors.
  Each owned id has its own look-for: a "create" id (E4-ALG-PS-01, E4-DAT-DI-10) is met by the student's own written
  algorithm or report made in the sitting or in counted draft and revision days, a "compare", "evaluate", "distinguish"
  or "analyze" id by a written judgment with its reason on new cases, never by recognition of a supplied answer, and a
  "debug" id (E4-PRO-TR-08) by a test-table the student fills while finding the piece that broke. Units 2 and 4 are
  assessed in the course-level check that follows them (Days). No score comes from a session that first teaches a
  row. The respond day reteaches by error letter and repeats the missed item in a new form.
- **Devices.** One device per pair is the default on a device day, never needed per child. Device days: unit 1
  session 1 (staged faults at stations), unit 3 sessions 1 and 2 (the table tool: records entered, a column sorted, a
  chart made), unit 4 sessions 1 to 5 (our own block editor with its text view and step-through trace), and unit 5
  session 1 (a one-screen sorter). That is 9 of the 22 teaching sessions, so a little over half the year's time is
  away from a device, about APPROACH.md's "about half" for grades 3 to 5 (ours, untested). Every device day is written
  four ways (STANDARD.md "Devices and rooms"): one device per pair, a few devices at stations with the other pairs on
  the paper version of the same idea, one screen with a student at the device for each step, or no device. One owned id
  needs a device to be met in full: E4-DAT-DC-09 asks for a computational tool (STANDARD.md "Devices and rooms"); on the
  no-device path unit 3 session 1 teaches records and attributes on the paper table, and the course record marks
  E4-DAT-DC-09 "part met without a device", never met. Every other id is met in full on paper: printed programs with
  trace-tables and test-tables and a partner acting as the computer meet E4-PRO-VD-06, E4-PRO-PD-04, E4-PRO-TR-08,
  E4-PRO-PD-05 and E4-PRO-RD-07 (a paper program with written comments is a program a team made and documented);
  picture fault cards and a toy device built from pictures meet E4-SYS-HW-12; a hand-drawn chart meets E4-DAT-DI-10;
  the class acting as the sorter meets E4-ALG-ML-02.
- **Paper path.** Every session runs on paper, cards and objects: printed programs with their text view, trace-tables
  and test-tables beside the editor; a paper data-table and a hand-drawn bar graph beside the table tool; the class as
  the sorter beside the one-screen sorter; picture fault cards beside the staged faults. No tool asks for a student
  account, stores a student's name or sends anything off the device; work is saved on the device or printed
  (STANDARD.md "Privacy and safety").
- **Reading level and vocabulary.** Grade 4 reads short instructions and programs of up to ten lines (APPROACH.md,
  Reading load); error messages are plain words we write. Each unit names five to eight words taught in depth, with the
  everyday words that have a computing meaning taught as two meanings (bug, run, table, record, train, network). The
  long words authentication and authorization come last in unit 1 session 3, after login and permission are sorted
  (progression read). Sentence frames: "First the program ___, then it ___" and "I predict ___ because line ___ says
  ___". A student may answer a trace in numbers, arrows or the home language, and each key says which wrong answers
  can come from reading the words rather than from the idea.
- **Swap or leave out.** STANDARD.md section 3 lets a school swap or leave out a named unit for its community's
  reasons. In this course every unit carries the only ids of its concept area, so units are swapped, not left out:
  unit 5's biographies may be swapped for people from the school's own community or region who added to computing (the
  same id, E4-SOC-HI-16, still with a dated source each), and its worker cases for local workplaces; unit 3's class
  question for another question about things. So the course always claims the data ids and the society ids (the bar
  part 5 fails a course with nothing on data or society). Units 1, 2 and 4 carry the troubleshooting steps, the written
  algorithm and the block program the rest of the year and grade 5 use and are not swapped. A school that leaves out
  a unit anyway names in the course page and the unit's family note the ids not claimed that year.
- **Safety.** No lab or field work, so no laboratory or field safety guide applies. Staged faults are set by the
  teacher with the device's own settings and its accessory cables (a mouse or headphone cable, the volume, a closed
  window, airplane mode); students never touch a power cord or a wall socket, never open a device's case, and never
  change a setting the teacher did not stage. Unit 1's acted-out network uses a string or yarn line held at waist
  height, walked, not run. Devices are carried with two hands and cords stay behind the table. The scope and sequence
  copies these lines into each station and device day.
- **Costs.** Provisional, for a class of 30, beyond the school's own devices: unit 1 $6 (printing fault cards and
  troubleshooting step cards, the two network-diagram mats, the door-and-rooms card sort, device-life strips; one ball
  of yarn), unit 2 $3 (printed choose-your-path algorithms and case cards, benefit-risk charts), unit 3 $4 (paper
  data-table sheets, graph paper, case cards), unit 4 $5 (printed programs with their text view, trace-table and
  test-table sheets for the paper path), unit 5 $4 (sorter picture cards, biography cards and the timeline strip, worker
  case cards), about $22 in all.
- **Sensitivity reads.** Every unit gets the fresh sensitivity agent's read (docs/pipeline/briefs/sensitivity-read.md);
  no outside reader is planned (Cory, 2026-11-14). Unit 1 has security and personal-information content: the agent
  checks the login-and-permission section carries health's "If a student tells you something" steps word for word
  (docs/courses/health/APPROACH.md; APPROACH.md, Sensitive ground), that every login is a pretend paper one, and that
  no card asks what devices a child has at home. Unit 2's two algorithms for picking teams and sharing a supply touch
  disability and being left out: before it reads, the agent studies the National Center on Disability and
  Journalism's Disability Language Style Guide (ncdj.org/style-guide/) and checks that talk stays on the algorithm and
  the case cards' invented people, never on a classmate. Unit 3 has data about people: the agent checks every class
  question is about things and every case is invented for practice. Unit 5 has Native, Black and women's history
  (Mary Golda Ross, of the Cherokee Nation; Katherine Johnson; Jerry Lawson; Grace Hopper) and work change that can
  touch a family: before it reads, the agent studies a starting pack: the Cherokee Nation's own pages (cherokee.org,
  the page the agent records), the Smithsonian National Museum of the American Indian (americanindian.si.edu), NASA's
  Katherine Johnson biography (nasa.gov), and the NCDJ guide above. The agent widens the pack to the brief's five
  sources, two of them owned by the communities whose people the cards show, and records them. The course's checks get
  the answer-key agent's read. No unit is hands-on in the laboratory sense, so no safety agent read is planned beyond
  the Safety lines above, which the sensitivity read confirms are on each day.

