## Units

| # | Unit and compelling question | Days | Skills | Owned standards | Practices and concept areas | What students learn | Why this unit, here | Check set adds | Sensitive topics and teacher notes |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | When it breaks and how it connects. *When a device stops working or cannot reach the internet, how do we find out why, and how do we keep it safe?* Anchor: the class's own device station on the first day, set up by the teacher with three staged faults (the sound is off, the mouse cable is out, the wrong window is open) | 6 | g4-troubleshoot; g4-wired-wireless; g4-authn-authz; g4-resources | E4-SYS-HW-12; E4-SYS-NT-14; E4-SYS-SE-13; E4-SYS-IM-15 | Concept area: systems and security. Practices: CT9 (check one thing at a time, test again); CT6 (say what is wrong before fixing it); ESR2 (proving who you are protects private information); ESR1 (who and what a device's life costs) | Session 1 (device, stations): a troubleshooting process in steps (what is wrong, is it plugged in, restart, try something simpler, ask an adult), shown first in a teacher think-aloud on one staged fault, then used by pairs on the staged faults (volume off, accessory cable out, wrong window or app open, airplane mode) until each is found and fixed; the system-diagram from grade 3 shows whether a fault sits in hardware or software. Session 2: two network-diagrams of the same room, one with cables to the router and one wireless, compared; each is better in one way (steady and fast; free to move), and the class acts out the signal along a yarn line and through the air. Session 3: a building with a front door and rooms: showing who you are at the door (a login, a password on a pretend paper account) is one thing, what you may open (a permission, a key for one room) is another; cards sorted as proving who someone is or deciding what they may do; the long words authentication and authorization given last. Session 4: a device traced on a strip from material (metals, plastics, energy in) to use to throwing away (waste out), with the benefit-risk-chart; how widely used systems affect resources and the environment, and one thing a user can do (use it longer, repair, recycle). Out: no fixes past the five steps (no settings beyond those staged, no opening a device, no installing), no network parts by name beyond device, cable, router and signal (grade 5's g5-network-parts names access point and modem), no real account or password, no confidentiality, integrity and availability (grade 5), no judging whether a device's cost is worth it. End-of-unit (day 5): put the steps in order for a new pictured fault and say the first check (E4-SYS-HW-12); for a new room, say which connection to use for two given needs and why (E4-SYS-NT-14); sort six new cards, login or permission, and explain one (E4-SYS-SE-13); trace a new device's life and name one effect and one user action (E4-SYS-IM-15); respond day 6. Tools: device stations (any school device with its own settings, job: the staged faults found and fixed). Curio Class writes: fault cards and the step cards, the two room network-diagrams, the door-and-rooms card sort and a pretend paper login, device-life strips with a dated teacher source line (the US Environmental Protection Agency's electronics donation and recycling page, epa.gov/recycle/electronics-donation-and-recycling, as of the day read) | First because the troubleshooting steps serve every device day after it (unit 3's table tool, unit 4's editor), and the year's first device session is the one with staged faults, run with the class's device rules from earlier grades. Networks, security and resources follow as the rest of the systems concept, all unplugged; resources closes the unit because it opens the benefit-risk-chart that unit 2 uses. Base: archived Year 5 "Systems and searching" lessons 1 and 2 for session 1's system-diagram retrieval (Source map) | Candidate: kept, the partial base's "systems of different sizes" input, process and output sort as the retrieval inside session 1, zero added days | Staged faults only, set by the teacher (Safety); no power cords or sockets. Login and permission: pretend paper accounts, no real password said or written; the teacher page carries health's "If a student tells you something" steps word for word. Some homes have no device and some have many: no student is asked what is at home. Recycling: no blame on families for how they dispose of devices. Agent reads: sensitivity. |
| 2 | Algorithms in words. *How do we write steps so anyone can follow them, and who do our steps help?* Anchor: a choose-your-path story written as numbered steps, where the reader's choice at step 4 sends them to step 7 or step 9 | 4 | g4-written-algorithm; g4-algorithm-groups | E4-ALG-PS-01; E4-ALG-IM-03 | Concept area: algorithms and design. Practices: CT8 (plan the steps before writing them out); CT7 (a pattern rule written once with a repeat); IC3 (a partner follows your written algorithm and says where it failed); ESR1 (who two algorithms help or disadvantage); HCD10 (case cards of people with different needs) | Session 1: the anchor story followed by every pair, then traced: which numbered line is the event that starts it, which lines repeat, which line is the choice and where each answer goes; grade 3's step-cards become written, numbered lines in everyday sentences. Session 2 (modify): a written algorithm for a number pattern (start at 1, add 3, stop at 22) rewritten with a repeat in place of the listed steps, then one line changed (a choice: if the number is even, clap) and checked by a partner following it. Session 3 (make): each pair writes its own numbered algorithm for a game, a route or a story with an event, a repeat and a choice; another pair follows it word for word and marks the first line that could not be followed; the writers fix it. Session 4: two algorithms for the same job (picking teams: captains choose in turn, or count off by fours; sharing a limited supply: first come first served, or an equal share for each), each laid out on step-cards and run on case cards of invented groups of people with different needs; a benefit-risk-chart for each; the class evaluates who each helps or disadvantages and why. Out: no flowchart, diagram or pseudocode (grade 5's g5-visual-algorithm), no programming language, no variable in the algorithm (grade 5 adds it), no designing a new, fairer algorithm (grade 4 compares; designing is past the row's depth), no pattern features beyond following the given rule (math grade 4 unit 7 explains features). End-of-unit: the mid-year course-level check (day 11) stands for it. Tools: none (all unplugged). Curio Class writes: the choose-your-path anchor and two more stories, the pattern algorithm, case cards of invented groups, step-cards for the two team and two supply algorithms | Second because every written algorithm and program after it reads numbered lines, and unit 4's programs are built from written plans; the choice and repeat here retrieve grade 3's g3-algorithm-combo before the editor uses them. Algorithm-groups closes the unit because it needs two algorithms students can now read and run, and uses unit 1's benefit-risk-chart. Our own (the partial base's Year 5 has no unplugged algorithm unit; its selection lessons are taught as programming in unit 4) | Candidate: kept, a reading-aloud partner step for students who write slowly in session 3, zero added days | Picking teams touches being chosen last and disability: the case cards are invented people, never classmates; teams are never picked in the room by skill during this session. Sharing a supply: no food. Talk stays on the algorithm's outcome for the cards' people. Agent reads: sensitivity (NCDJ guide first). |
| 3 | Tables, charts and people. *Which kind of book fills our class library most, and who could data about people help or hurt?* Anchor: the class library's books in a heap, and a question nobody can answer by looking | 6 | g4-table-tool; g4-data-explain; g4-data-people | E4-DAT-DC-09; E4-DAT-DI-10; E4-DAT-IM-11 | Concept area: data and analysis. Practices: CT6 (pose a question data can answer); CT7 (a pattern in the chart); IC3 (explain the process and the result to a reader); ESR1 and ESR2 (who is helped or hurt by data about people; no real student data) | Session 1 (device): the class counts its library books into a paper data-table, one row a record (a book) and one column an attribute (kind, number of pages, fiction or not); then pairs enter twelve records into the table tool and sort one column, and say why each row is one book. Session 2 (device or paper): from a question the class asks of its own table (which kind of book do we have most of? are longer books mostly fiction?), pairs make a bar graph or chart from the table in the tool, or draw it by hand on graph paper with a scale of 1, 2 or 5 (grade 3 math's scaled graphs, retrieved). Session 3: each student writes a short report with the chart attached: the question, how the data was collected and entered, what the chart shows, one thing it cannot tell us; a partner gives one written comment against three look-fors and the writer revises. Session 4: case cards of data collected about groups of people (a school survey of how students travel to school, a map of where people walk in a park, a store's count of shoppers by hour), all invented for practice; students ask who collects it, what is collected and why, then say which person and which group could be helped or hurt, in plain words (helpful, harmful, unfair), on a data-table of the cases. Out: no search or filter with two conditions (grade 6's g6-sort-filter-tool), no data of more than one type collected with a tool (grade 5), no spread or variability (grade 5), no weighing whether a collection is good or bad overall (row depth), no real data about students. End-of-unit (day 17): enter six new records into the table tool and sort a column, or on paper (marked part met, Devices) (E4-DAT-DC-09); from a new small table, make a chart and write three sentences on the process and the result (E4-DAT-DI-10; session 3's revised report is read beside it, unscored); a new case card: who collects, what and why, who could be helped and who hurt (E4-DAT-IM-11); respond day 18. Tools: a table tool (rows and columns, sort a column, a bar chart from a column, saves on the device or prints; tool ask for TOOL-ASKS.md). Curio Class writes: the data-table sheet, the report look-fors with a meets and a not-yet sample, the case cards, a twelve-record practice set for a room without a library | After unit 2 because the report is a written explanation in steps, and before unit 4 because the quiz program keeps a score as a value students can now see in a table. Before unit 5, whose training-set is a table of labeled examples. Base: archived Year 5 "Flat-file databases" lessons 1, 2, 3, 5 and 6 (Source map) | Candidate: kept, the partial base's paper-database start (records on cards before the tool) inside session 1, zero added days | Class data is about things (books), never about students, homes or bodies. The travel-to-school case is invented and asks no student how they travel. Some students have few books at home: the data is the class library only. Agent reads: sensitivity. |
| 4 | The class quiz, built in pieces. *How do we build a program together so it works and anyone can read it?* Anchor: a five-question class quiz program that keeps a score, shown working, then shown again broken after someone added three new questions at once | 5 | g4-trace-values; g4-compare-solutions; g4-debug-increment; g4-team-document | E4-PRO-VD-06; E4-PRO-PD-04; E4-PRO-TR-08; E4-PRO-PD-05; E4-PRO-RD-07 | Concept area: programming. Practices: CT9 (test after each new piece); CT7 (a repeat in place of copied blocks); CT8 (compare two approaches); IC4 (a team plan with each member's part and a check-off); IC5 (driver and navigator roles that switch every eight minutes); IC3 (a comment written for a reader, checked by one) | Session 1 (device and paper): predict, then run, then compare: students trace how values flow through a counting loop and through the quiz's score program, filling a trace-table (which value goes in, how the score and the counter change after each line, what comes out) and checking with the editor's step-through; "set score to score plus 1" is read as a change to the box, not a math statement. Grade 3's choice (if the answer is right) is retrieved here. Session 2 (device): two given programs reach the same result in different ways (a long one with copied blocks and a short one with a repeat; two quiz scorers, one with a clear name for its variable and one without); students run both, check both give the right result on three tests, and say which is clearer for a new reader and why. Session 3 (device, modify): the anchor's broken build is rebuilt one piece at a time, tested after each new question with a test-table (what I tested, what I expected, what happened), and the bug is found because the last piece is the only change; then a second given buggy build. Session 4 (device, make): teams of three plan the class quiz on paper (each member's two questions, the score, the end message), then build it with driver and navigator roles that switch on the timer; each member adds a part, tests it with the test-table and writes a short comment on what it does. Session 5 (device, feedback and revision): a reader from another team checks each comment against the code and writes one note; the team revises the code or the comment and runs every test again. Ten lines a part at most. Out: no new constructs beyond sequence, event, repeat, a choice and one score variable (comparing a variable with a number to decide is grade 5's g5-variables-use), no remixing another team's program (grade 5's g5-remix-program), no strategies beyond testing piece by piece (grade 5 compares three), no external documentation beyond the comment. End-of-unit: the year-end course-level check (day 24) stands for it. Tools: our own block editor (blocks and a text view of the same program, a step-through trace, runs stated tests and shows yes or not yet, saves on the device; one file, no account; STANDARD.md "Our own editor"). Curio Class writes: the anchor quiz and its broken build, the counting-loop and score programs with their trace-table keys, the two pairs of programs to compare, the buggy builds with their test-table keys, the team plan sheet and the comment look-fors, printed versions of every program with the text view for the paper path, and a finished, tested quiz for the teacher page | After unit 3 because the score is a value the class has already read as data, and after unit 2 because the team plans its quiz as written numbered steps first. The order inside is the subject's: trace before compare, compare and modify before the team makes, and the team's feedback session before the score (lesson 19). After ELA grade 4 unit 2, which teaches explaining a procedure (see Cross-subject). Placed across weeks 19 to 23, which include the core check weeks 19, 21 and 23, because it holds no course-level check until week 24. Base: archived Year 5 "Selection in quizzes", all six lessons (Source map) | Candidate: kept, the partial base's "evaluating a quiz" step folded into session 5's reader check, zero added days | Pair and team roles switch on the timer so no student holds the keys all period; a student who cannot use the keyboard or mouse takes the navigator role with a typed or list choice in the editor, never sits out. Quiz questions are about class topics, never about classmates. Agent reads: sensitivity. |
| 5 | Machines that learn, and people who make tools. *How is the way a machine learns different from the way we learn, and who has made the tools we use?* Anchor: a picture sorter on one screen that calls every red shape a circle, because every circle it was shown was red | 7 | g4-ml-learning; g4-history-limits; g4-work-tools | E4-ALG-ML-02; E4-SOC-HU-18; E4-SOC-HI-16; E4-SOC-ET-17; E4-SOC-CE-19 | Concept area: algorithms and design (machine learning) with computing and society. Practices: CT7 (a pattern in the training examples); IC3 (explain why the output changed); HCD10 (whose contributions shaped a tool); ESR1 (who gains when a tool improves, and who must learn something new) | Session 1 (one-screen device and unplugged): change one property of a training-set (all one color; too few examples; one kind missing), predict how the sorter's output will change, then observe it on the one screen and with the class acting as the sorter on picture cards; the relationship between what the examples are like and what the sorter outputs is the point. Session 2: compare how a child learns a new word (meaning, a few examples, asking why) and how a machine learns from many labeled examples with no understanding; a two-column chart and a written sentence of each kind. Session 3: biography cards of diverse people and communities in the history of computing go on the timeline with what each added: Grace Hopper (programs written in words, not only numbers), Katherine Johnson (the calculations for early American spaceflight), Mary Golda Ross of the Cherokee Nation (an engineer of early spacecraft design), Jerry Lawson (a game console that took changeable game cartridges), and the women who first programmed one of the earliest electronic computers; each card has a dated teacher source line (Sources, below). Session 4: name a limit of an existing tool (a room-sized computer, a battery that runs out, a screen too small to share) and how that limit led people to make something new, with the timeline and a benefit-risk-chart. Session 5: case cards of workers whose jobs changed when a new computing tool arrived (an office, a farm with soil sensors, a clinic with electronic records); students say what each worker had to learn and how they kept learning. Out: no training on a device by students (grade 5's g5-train-model), no faces, voices or named people in any training set, no claim that a machine thinks or understands, no deciding when a technology is the right tool (grade 5's g5-use-or-not), no product or company names, no dates to memorize. Sources: NASA's Katherine Johnson biography (nasa.gov), the Naval History and Heritage Command's Grace Hopper page (history.navy.mil), the United States Mint's page on the 2019 Native American one-dollar coin honoring Mary Golda Ross (usmint.gov), the Computer History Museum on Jerry Lawson and on the first programmers (computerhistory.org), and the Bureau of Labor Statistics Occupational Outlook Handbook for the worker cases (bls.gov/ooh), each opened and dated by the unit writer; the worker cases' people are invented for practice. End-of-unit (day 31): a new training-set with one property changed, predict and explain the output (E4-ALG-ML-02); one written difference between how a person and a machine learn (E4-SOC-HU-18); place a new biography card and say what the person or community added (E4-SOC-HI-16); name a pictured tool's limit and a new tool it led to (E4-SOC-ET-17); a new worker case, what they had to learn (E4-SOC-CE-19); respond day 32. Tools: a picture sorter (one screen, the teacher's picture sets, shows each output and its training examples; tool ask for TOOL-ASKS.md). Curio Class writes: the sorter's picture sets and the paper sorter cards, the learning comparison chart, the five biography cards and the timeline strip, the limit cards, the worker case cards | Last because the training-set is a table of labeled examples (unit 3) and the society sessions gather the year's tools. Its rows need nothing taught after them and hand on only to grade 5, so it can follow the year-end check, which the core check weeks 27 and 30 to 32 hold at week 24. Machine learning sits with the society rows because its own row teaches the algorithm and the human-learning comparison as one story. Our own (the partial base's Year 5 has no machine learning or society unit) | Candidate: dropped, a visit by a local worker who uses a computing tool; the cases carry the row and a visit needs a school's own arrangement, zero days | Native content (Mary Golda Ross, Cherokee Nation): told as her own work, from the nation's and the agencies' own pages, never as a symbol. Black history (Katherine Johnson, Jerry Lawson) and women's history: contributions, not barriers alone. Work change can touch a family whose job changed or was lost: no student is asked about a family member's work; the cases are invented people. Agent reads: sensitivity (the starting pack in Sensitivity reads first). |

