## 5. Cost, devices and rooms

Programming units run on a device per one or two students in a common browser; nothing is installed and no account is
made (bar criterion 7). Physical computing (a microcontroller board) is optional and always has a simulator or paper
stand-in; its cost is stated where used.

### Devices and rooms

Three things are kept apart. An unplugged day teaches an idea with cards, paper or movement; every unit has at least
one (kind: understanding; bar item 5). A device day is a day where a tool does a named job. A stand-in is how a device
day runs with fewer devices (C14). Every device day is written four ways (section fields 5 and 10): (1) one device per
pair, the default; (2) a few devices: three or four stations on a rotation, the other pairs at a paper station on the
same idea, every pair taking the keyboard role at least once; (3) one screen: the class runs the program together,
predicts and votes before each run, and a student at the device takes each step; (4) no device: the same idea on paper,
with trace tables, "be the computer", programs written on paper and checked by a partner. An id whose standard asks
students to use a computational tool, train a model, or use documentation and libraries is met only on a device (for
example E4-DAT-DC-09, E5-DAT-DC-09, E5-ALG-ML-02, MS-DAT-DC-23, MS-DAT-DI-25, MS-PRO-PD-13, HS-DAT-DC-21,
HS-DAT-DC-23, HS-ALG-ML-08, HS-PRO-PD-12, HS-PRO-PD-13, HS-SYS-HW-30). On the no-device path that day teaches the
idea and the course record marks the id "part met without a device"; the course never says the id was met.

### Generated-text tools

Evaluating generated output (MS-PRO-RD-18, HS-PRO-RD-18, HS-ALG-PS-05) is met on supplied material: Curio Class supplies
saved outputs and code samples, some right and some flawed, and students judge them for accuracy and use. No tool
asks a student to use a live generated-text tool, make an account or send anything off the device. For MS-ALG-PS-05,
which asks students to use one (Cory, 2026-11-20, Desk q-prework-cs-1): no live tool in Curio
Class; the day is teacher-run on one screen with a tool the school has already approved and no student accounts, and
the course marks the id "part met" where a school has none.

### Our own editor

Settled (Cory, 2026-11-21, Desk q-prework-open-1): build one small browser editor of our own (blocks and a
text view, with a step-through trace), a single file with no account, starting with grades 3 to 5, rather than pointing
teachers to a third-party tool. The text language for grades 6 to 12 is Python, run in this editor (Cory, 2026-11-21,
Desk q-fix-cs-1): the K to 8 partial base teaches Python in its middle years, so its units adapt directly, and it is
the most common first text language in high school.

The editor also keeps a small offline version history with branch, review and merge, so grade 12's team project
meets S1-SWD-PP-08 in full; until that exists, the standard is part met and the course page says so (Cory, 2026-11-21,
Desk q-fix-cs-2). It is an ask for the editor's own build list when the editor is specced.

### Blocks to text

Kindergarten to grade 5: block code with a text view of the same program. Grade 6: blocks and Python text side by
side. Grade 7: written Python for new programs, blocks as a stand-in. Grade 8 on: Python. Python is named as a subject
term on teacher pages and runs in our own editor in a common browser with nothing installed. The language is Cory's
(above); the grade steps are ours, untested.

### Privacy and safety

No student's name, photo, voice, location or family detail goes into a program, a dataset or a tool. Class data is about
things (weather, pets drawn from a list, shapes, snack types), tallied by hand or with counts, never about identifiable
children, health or home. Accounts, passwords and personal information are taught with a pretend page or a paper account;
no student makes an account. Online safety days use paper or an offline pretend site; any real web use is teacher-led on
one screen. Security days use toy systems built into the page; no scanning, no attack and no test of a real school or
home system, ever. A tool stores student work only on the device or in a file the student downloads or prints; it sends
nothing off the device.

### Real cases in society days

A real case is told by what happened, not by the company or product: "a company sold a face-matching system to police;
a 2018 study found it wrong more often for darker-skinned women" (example only, from memory, facts to be checked
before use). The teacher page gives the date and the source's title and publisher in a source line, which is a citation
and not a promotion. If a case cannot be understood without the name, choose another case or use a court or government
record's own description. A society day gives two views and one dated source for each.
Decided (Cory, 2026-11-20, Desk q-prework-cs-1): student and teacher copy may name government bodies and
courts but never companies, with sources only in the teacher source line.
**Unsupported.** A screen cannot give movement time. An open site honestly offers plans, task cards, assessments
and knowledge content.

**Standards.** data/standards/shape-pe-2024.txt (four standards). The federal tool is keyed to the earlier five. The
method carries over; the tables do not. Decided 2026-11-11 (card q-standards-currency): the 2024 standards are
kept, with a written mapping to the older-keyed review tool.

**Scope and sequence states:** the outcome for each standard by grade. **Section plan fills:** space, equipment and
the adaptation. **Confidence:** likely.

### 5. Computer science

**Rests on.** Ohio's rubric (April 2025): gateway 1 read in full by the writer, all 24 pages by the expert.

1. [S] Coverage spans every concept area, not programming alone: systems, data, algorithms, society.
2. [S] The practices (working together, testing and fixing, abstraction, explaining) are taught inside the content.
3. [I] Programming ideas are ordered, and each band says what the one before taught.
4. [I] Students meet real, current uses and cases of computing.
5. [O] Every unit has unplugged work. Ohio asks about offline versions and gives them no points.
6. [I] Technology is built into the learning, not added on: each tool has a purpose in the lesson, gives feedback,
   and does not distract. This is where Ohio's first gateway puts most of its points.
7. [I] Tools run in a common browser on school devices, with notes for technical staff. [O] Nothing to install.
8. [I] Assessment includes projects and code, with rubrics.
9. [I] Teacher pages assume a teacher not trained in the subject and explain the content at adult level.

**Fails if:** block puzzles with no concepts named; nothing on data or society; a tool that needs an install or an
account; no help for the non-specialist.

**Unsupported.** No national materials rubric. No page may claim coding improves general thinking.

**Standards.** Loaded (data/standards/): the 196 Foundational ids, the 135 Specialty ids and the statements; see docs/courses/computer-science/STANDARD.md. The four high school courses teach the Foundational set and claim a
Specialty standard only where a topic already goes that far (decided 2026-11-11, card q-standards-currency): eight S1 ids, four in grade 11 and four in grade 12 (Cory, 2026-11-20, Desk q-prework-cs-1; named in docs/courses/computer-science/STANDARD.md).

**Scope and sequence states:** the concept areas per unit. **Section plan fills:** the unplugged task.
**Confidence:** likely.

### 6. World languages: Spanish, American Sign Language. Shelved, 2026-11-11

def main(a):
    if not a:
        raise SystemExit(__doc__)
    cmd, rest = a[0], a[1:]
    if cmd == 'check':
        bad = 0
        import sequence   # scope and sequence stage (added 2026-11-14)
        for c in (rest[:1] or courses()):
            if sequence.stage(c) == 'scope':
                fl, nt = sequence.check(c)
                print('sequence %s: %d failure(s)' % (c, len(fl)))
                for x in fl: print('  FAIL', x)
                for x in nt: print('  note', x)
                bad += len(fl)
                continue
            # a course whose days.csv keeps the scope layout (one row per day of the year, an assess column) while its
            # first unit is planned keeps its scope gate (social studies probe, us-history-i unit 5, 2026-11-15)
            if 'assess' in (rows(c, 'days')[:1] or [{}])[0]:
                fl, nt = sequence.check(c)
                print('sequence %s: %d failure(s)' % (c, len(fl)))
                for x in fl: print('  FAIL', x)
                bad += len(fl)
            fails, notes, ni, nw = check(c)
            print('%s: %d items and %d wrong answers checked, %d failure(s)' % (c, ni, nw, len(fails)))
            for x in fails:
                print('  FAIL', x)
            for x in notes:
                print('  note', x)
            bad += len(fails)
            ng = new_gates(c)
            for x in ng:
                print('  FAIL', x)
            bad += len(ng)
        if not rest:
            import progression
            for s in [s for s in progression.STD_FILES if progression.load(s) is not None]:
                pf, pn, n = progression.check(s)
                print('progression %s: %d rows, %d failure(s)' % (s, n, len(pf)))
                for x in pf: print('  FAIL', x)
                for x in pn: print('  note', x)
                bad += len(pf)
            import frame   # course frames (added 2026-11-13)
            cal = frame.calendar_rows()
            gf = frame.check_global()
            print('frames: %d course(s), %d catalogue failure(s)' % (len(frame.found()), len(gf)))
            for x in gf: print('  FAIL', x)
            bad += len(gf)
            for s in frame.found():
                ff, fn = frame.check(s, cal)
                print('frame %s: %d failure(s)' % (s, len(ff)))
                for x in ff: print('  FAIL', x)
                for x in fn: print('  note', x)
                bad += len(ff)
        sys.exit(1 if bad else 0)
    elif cmd == 'tables':
        for c in (rest[:1] or courses()):
            tables(c)
    elif cmd == 'pack':
        pack(rest[0], str(int(rest[1])))
    elif cmd == 'defect':
        defect_add(rest)
    elif cmd == 'reserve':
        reserve(rest[0], str(int(rest[1])))
    elif cmd == 'defects':
        defects()
    else:
        raise SystemExit(__doc__)


if __name__ == '__main__':
    main(sys.argv[1:])
"""Stale-note catch (added 2026-11-13): notes that say a piece of work is not done yet go stale the moment it is.

    python -X utf8 scripts/stale.py check [file ...]

A note that waits on other work carries a marker the checker can test:

    Pending(<what>): <the note>

where <what> is one of
- a file path from the repository root: resolved once the file exists;
- path#text: resolved once that file contains the text (Pending(data/standards/x.txt#RST.11-12.8));
- a plan-flow node id (frame:science:5, prog-review:all): resolved once data/plans/flow-status.csv marks it done.

FAIL: a Pending marker whose work has landed (the note is stale: rewrite it to say what is now true and drop the
marker); a Pending marker naming no file, text or node the checker knows the form of; a work-status phrase
("not yet placed", "until it is added", "to come", "TBD", "placeholder" and the like) outside a Pending marker
(mark it so the checker can follow it, or reword it).
Teaching boundaries ("not yet describe", "not yet measured with a ruler") are about the student, not the work, and
are not flagged. With no files, checks the four progressions and the course frames (docs/courses/**/*.md,
data/frames/*.csv when they exist).
"""
import csv
import glob
import os
import re
import sys

ROOT = os.path.dirname(os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)))
MARK = re.compile(r'Pending\(([^)]*)\)\s*:')
# Work-status wording: says something about our own unfinished work. Kept narrow so a student boundary
# ("not yet describe") never trips it.
WORK = re.compile(
    r"not yet (in the|placed|written|built|added|loaded|in a curio class|named by|decided|chosen)"
    r"|until (it|they|this|that|the [\w ,'-]{1,40}) (is|are) (added|written|built|loaded|placed|decided|chosen)"
    r"|once (it|they|the [\w ,'-]{1,40}) (is|are|exists?) (added|written|built|loaded|placed|decided)"
    r"|\b(?:yet|still|is|are) to come\b|\bTBD\b|\bTODO\b|placeholder|will be (placed|written|added|decided)"
    r"|held here in words", re.I)
_done = None


def done_nodes():
    global _done
    if _done is None:
        _done = set()
        p = os.path.join(ROOT, 'data', 'plans', 'flow-status.csv')

