solve real-world problems.



Boundary Statement: Students should investigate and describe examples of computational thinking

across a range of professions. Students should be able to connect computational thinking concepts

such as abstraction, decomposition, and pattern recognition to specific job functions, identifying

parallels between how they think when coding and how professionals think when solving domain-

specific problems.



Students are not expected to explore specialized programming frameworks or conduct domain-

specific simulations that require advanced mathematics, physics, or proprietary software.



Practice(s): IC4, CT6             Disposition(s): Sense of Belonging, Creativity



MS-SOC-CE-45: Evaluate how automation in technology can create or replace jobs and

change how people work.



