## For the lead

1. **After-core check weeks (calendar pass).** This course's checks sit in weeks 11 and 24, off the grade 4 core
   check weeks 8 to 10, 12, 16, 19, 21, 23, 27 and 30 to 32. In docs/courses/CALENDAR.md, grade 4 week 24 holds music's
   year-end check and week 11 holds no after-core check; they stay for the lead's one calendar pass, under the pick in
   the kindergarten frame's For the lead 1. Week 24 rather than 25, 26, 28 or 29 is chosen so unit 5 has seven weeks;
   if the pass needs week 25 or 26, unit 5 loses one or two teaching sessions (session 4 joins session 3, then session
   2 joins session 1), and the lead says which.
2. **Year 5 of the partial base.** The pilot maps Year 1 to kindergarten, so Year 5 is mapped here to grade 4. The
   Year 5 lessons were placed from their titles and unit overviews on the archived pages (2026-11-25), not from the
   lesson files; the placement is checked when the store folder is made (pilot For the lead 4). Selection, the main
   construct of the two Year 5 programming units, is grade 3's here (g3-algorithm-combo, E3-PRO-PD-04), so "Selection
   in quizzes" is used for its quiz-building and testing order, with selection as review; "Selection in physical
   computing" is left out (it needs a microcontroller board, and its idea is grade 3's). "Flat-file databases" lesson
   4 (search tools with conditions) is offered to the grade 6 frame (g6-sort-filter-tool).
3. **Web search lessons.** "Systems and searching" lessons 3 to 6 (searching the web, choosing and ranking results)
   carry no E4 id; search and judging sources are the library course's method (STANDARD.md section 4). Pick: offer them
   to the library grade 4 frame as a lean, not adapted here.
4. **Our own editor and two small tools.** STANDARD.md "Our own editor" starts the editor with grades 3 to 5. Unit 4
   needs from it: a step-through trace that shows each variable's value after each line, stated tests with yes or not
   yet, a comment block that shows in the text view, and a typed or list choice in place of drag and drop. Units 3
   and 5 need two small tools for TOOL-ASKS.md: a table tool (rows, columns, sort, a bar chart, nothing sent off the
   device) and a one-screen picture sorter with changeable training sets. The paper path meets every grade 4 id in
   full except E4-DAT-DC-09, which is part met without a device (Devices).
5. **Source list.** docs/APPROACH-SOURCES.md, beside the pilot's entries: the archived key stage 2 unit pages
   (raspberrypi.org/curriculum/archived/key-stage-2), the EPA electronics donation and recycling page, NASA's Katherine
   Johnson biography, the Naval History and Heritage Command on Grace Hopper, the US Mint's 2019 Native American dollar
   page, the Computer History Museum, the Bureau of Labor Statistics Occupational Outlook Handbook, and the Cherokee
   Nation's own pages. Each is opened and dated by the unit writer; any that refuses automated reads is kept as
   "browser check" (AGENTS.md).
6. **Row changes.** None needed: every grade 4 row is placed as written, and both ends of every handoff already match
   the grade 3 and grade 5 rows.

## Source map

The partial base for grade 4 is the archived edition of England's Teach Computing Curriculum, Year 5 (six units;
raspberrypi.org/curriculum/archived/key-stage-2, resources dated 2023). Placed from the public unit pages (lesson
titles and unit overviews), opened 2026-11-25; each archived Year 5 unit lists six lessons. The publisher teaches each
unit's lessons in number order; where this frame splits a unit, reorders it or takes part of it, the row says why.
Each adapted lesson keeps its idea, is rewritten in our words for an American grade 4, replaces the named block
language, database program and microcontroller with our own editor, table tool and paper versions, and is credited
once a day. Check days are ours, never a kept lesson.

