## Cross-subject

All leans are to grade 6 courses in the same year, which meet every school day, so their course days are their
school days (data/plans/calendar.csv); this course's day n falls on school day 2.5 n, rounded up.

### Mathematics, grade 6

| Row | Lean | Used here | Earliest math unit that teaches it | Bridge if math teaches it later |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| g6-variable-types | m6-expressions-variables (6.EE.A.2: expressions with letters standing for numbers) | Unit 1, sessions 1 to 7 (weeks 1 to 4) | math grade 6 unit 6 (days 96 to 117, weeks 20 to 24) | Math teaches it later. The unit teaches its own variable as a named box that holds one value and can change, and names the math meaning beside it (a letter that stands for a number in an expression); no unit 1 task writes or evaluates an algebraic expression, and the arithmetic is whole numbers and tenths from grade 5 |
| g6-model-algorithm | m6-expressions-variables (6.EE.A.2) | Unit 2, sessions 1 to 4 (weeks 9 to 11) | math grade 6 unit 6 (days 96 to 117) | Math teaches it later. Variables appear in a model only where the logic needs them (a count of late books), named as unit 1's boxes; no expression is simplified or solved |
| g6-sort-filter-tool | m6-summarize-data (6.SP.B.5: summaries of a data set in context) | Unit 3, sessions 5 to 7 (weeks 16 and 17) | math grade 6 unit 8 (days 142 to 157, weeks 29 to 32) | Math teaches it later. The summaries are count, total, smallest and largest, and the average as the total shared out evenly, worked with grade 5's whole-number and decimal division; median, spread and the mean as a measure of center stay with math unit 8, and no task asks which summary describes the data best |

### Science, grade 6

| Row | Lean | Used here | Earliest science unit that teaches it | Bridge if science teaches it later |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| g6-packets | s6-digital-signals (MS-PS4-3: digital signals carry information more reliably than analog) | Unit 5, sessions 1 to 3 (weeks 24 and 25) | science grade 6 unit 5 (days 116 to 135, weeks 24 to 27) | Taught in the same weeks, so either may come first. This unit needs only that a message can be written as ones and zeros: the code card does the encoding, and no session claims why digital signals are more reliable, which stays with science; if science has started, session 1 names the link in one line |

No row leans on English language arts or social studies. Writing an explanation of a program or a claim with two
views (units 1, 2 and 6) and reading the era source pack (unit 6) are practiced here, not owned.

## For the lead

1. **After-core check weeks (calendar pass).** This course's checks sit on school days 95 (week 19) and 158 (week 32,
   the finals week), off every grade 6 core check day. Overlaps with other after-core subjects' check days are left
   for the lead's one calendar pass, as the brief says; `frame.py calendar` lists them after sync.
2. **Row changes from the boundary read.** The grade 6 owner rows were read against their boundary statements in
   the source store for this frame; six rows' notes are narrower than the boundary or carry UK spelling, and the frame
   already teaches the wider depth. The changes are listed in the final report under Row changes; the progression is
   the lead's to edit.
3. **Grade 7 seam.** The grade 5 frame's Handed on table and this frame's Picked up table name the same rows. The
   grade 7 frame (docs/courses/computer-science-grade-7/frame.md) leaves the grade 6 unit numbers to the seam read;
   this frame's Handed on table gives them (units 1 to 6), and its leave-out line for unit 4 means three grade 7 rows
   then open with a readiness check.
4. **The partial base store folder.** As the kindergarten pilot's For the lead 4: the Year 7 units mapped here exist
   only in the archived edition; the lead loads them with the rest of the archive into
   S:\CurioClass-sources\teach-computing-curriculum, reads each file's own license line, and the per-unit third-party
   check runs then. The lesson-level topics of the block-language part II lessons 7 to 9 are read from the unit
   overview (subroutines, decomposition, iteration) and confirmed from the lesson files when loaded.
5. **Our own editor, grade 6 view (scope, Cory's).** STANDARD.md "Our own editor" starts the editor with grades 3 to
   5; grade 6 needs blocks and Python text side by side, both editable, with a step-through trace and stated tests.
   Pick: add the side-by-side mode to the editor's build list for grade 6. The paper path meets MS-PRO-VD-16 and
   MS-PRO-PD-12 in full without it, so the course does not wait on it.
6. **Three toy tools for TOOL-ASKS.md.** A table tool (sort, filter, group, summarize, one chart), a toy classifier
   with its training set visible, and a toy network with packets and a crowded-link switch; the sensor loop of unit 6
   may be a mode of the network tool. Each runs in one file with no account and sends nothing off the device.
7. **Source list.** docs/APPROACH-SOURCES.md, beside the kindergarten pilot's entries: the archived key stage 3
   address (raspberrypi.org/curriculum/archived/key-stage-3), the Bureau of Labor Statistics Occupational Outlook
   Handbook (bls.gov/ooh), the census.gov history pages on the 1890 tabulation, NASA's biographies of Katherine
   Johnson and Dorothy Vaughan, DARPA's own ARPANET page (darpa.mil), IMLS public library reports (imls.gov) and the
   FTC's children's privacy guidance (ftc.gov).

## Source map

The partial base for grade 6 is the archived edition of England's Teach Computing Curriculum, Year 7 (six units;
raspberrypi.org/curriculum/archived/key-stage-3, resources dated 2023). Placed from the public unit pages (lesson
titles and unit overviews), opened 2026-11-25; each archived Year 7 unit lists six lessons, the second programming
unit numbered 7 to 12. The publisher teaches each unit's lessons in number order; where this frame splits or reorders
a unit, the row says why. Each adapted lesson keeps its idea, is rewritten in our words for an American grade 6,
replaces the named block language and spreadsheet program with our own editor and table tool, and is credited once a
day. Check days are ours, never a kept lesson.

| Base unit (Year 7) | Lessons | Placed in | How it is used | Left out, and why |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Programming essentials, block language, part I | 1 Introduction to programming and sequencing; 2 Sequence and variables; 3 Selection; 4 Operators; 5 Count-controlled iteration; 6 Problem-solving | Unit 1, sessions 1 to 7 | Lessons 1 and 2: the worked program and variables (sessions 1 and 2); lesson 4: comparing and joining values (session 3); lesson 3: a choice on a variable (session 4); lesson 5: the count-controlled repeat (sessions 5 and 6); lesson 6: the make session (session 7). Lesson 3 follows lesson 4 here so values are changed and combined before a choice reads them; each runs in both views, which the base does not | None |
| Programming essentials, block language, part II | 7 You've got the moves!; 8 Fly cat, fly!; 9 Loop the loop! | Unit 1, sessions 6 and 8 to 10 | Lessons 7 and 8: making and calling a subroutine, and decomposition (sessions 8 to 10, from the unit overview); lesson 9: iteration, as review inside session 6 | None of 7 to 9 |
| Programming essentials, block language, part II | 10 Treasure those lists!; 11 Translate this! Part 1; 12 Translate this! Part 2 | None | | All three left out: lists are grade 7's (g7-lists), and MS-PRO-VD-16 says collections are not expected; the lists project is offered to the grade 7 frame |
| Modelling data using spreadsheets | 1 Getting to know a spreadsheet; 2 Quick calculations; 4 Become a data master! | Unit 3, sessions 5 to 7 | Lesson 1: the table tool and a worked sort and filter (session 5); lesson 2: summaries (session 6); lesson 4: answering questions with the tool's steps (session 7) | None of 1, 2 and 4 |
| Modelling data using spreadsheets | 3 Collecting data; 5 Level up your data skills!; 6 Assessment | None | | Lesson 3 left out: MS-DAT-DC-23 asks for no collecting, and collecting with a tool is grade 5's; lesson 5 (charts) left out beyond session 7's one summary chart: chart design choices are grade 7's g7-viz-choices; lesson 6: check days are ours |
| Networks: from semaphores to the internet | 3 Packets and layers; 4 Routing; 5 Performance; 6 Privacy | Unit 5, sessions 1 to 4 | Lesson 3: packets (session 1), its layers left out; lesson 4: routing in the packet game (sessions 1 and 3); lesson 5: loss and congestion (session 2); lesson 6: protecting information on a network, as the triangle cases (session 4) | Layers inside lesson 3: protocol detail is not expected at this depth |
| Networks: from semaphores to the internet | 1 Networking hardware; 2 Addresses and protocols | None | | Both left out: network parts are grade 5's (g5-network-parts, met as session 1's retrieval), the roles of routers and servers are grade 7's MS-SYS-NT-35 (g7-internet-resilience), and protocols are not expected; offered to the grade 7 frame |
| Clear messaging in digital media | Lessons 1 to 6 | None | | All six left out: poster, brand and slide making, no CSTA id; outside bar C1's 10 percent if kept |
| Using media: gaining support for a cause | Lessons 1 to 6 | None | | All six left out: a blog made in a media app, no grade 6 id; judging sources is the library course's method and crediting others' work is grade 8's MS-PRO-PD-14 (g8-attribution), so lessons 2 and 3 are offered to those frames |

Units 1 (sessions 11 to 16, the team program), 2, 3 (sessions 1 to 4 and 8 to 10), 4, 5 (session 5) and 6 are our
