20. **State adoption is the bar we build to.** Full-course materials (units, Tracks, the Binder and the shelves that
    feed them) are built to meet the substance of state and national curriculum reviews. We do not pursue formal
    adoption now; if later data says it is a good path, we pivot and are already well placed. The bar never overrides
    "What we will not do" in docs/VISION.md (no accounts, tracking or student analytics). What the bar asks, what we
    took and what we left out: docs/ADOPTION-BAR.md.
21. **A tool teaches a standard only when it passes the teaching test.** A student alone, following the tool's lead,
    meets a model, does the standard's own verb (solve, write, explain, record) on every part of its wording, on five
    or more items that differ, with a check where thinking is cheaper than guessing, and none of it can be skipped.
    For writing and talk a tool cannot mark, a model, a guide while working and a self-check on screen against a shown
    guide count. Every tool states a role: `teach` (passes the test), `see why` (lets a student explore the idea and
    names what the unit adds) or `practice`. A tool claims a code only for work on its main path; a readout, an
    optional mode or a teacher note never carries a code. Recess games and Curiosities take the role `recess` or
    `curiosity` and claim no code.
22. **Topics name standards.** A topic page covers one teachable topic. It is split only when it holds two unrelated
    topics, and then properly: a split script, redirects for the old pages, and its Word Wall, chart and games divided
    by hand (its id keys all of them and about 762 live pages; docs/ECOSYSTEM.md). It is never split just to reach
    standard-level detail; the ledger does that. Split candidates come to Cory as exceptions. A topic's standards are
    spelled out in place, never a bare cluster, each tool carries its own codes, and data/coverage.csv records where
    every standard is taught in full, so an untaught standard shows. The section plan decides how many tools a unit
    gets (rule 23); a major unit may run on print with no key tool (grade 4 math unit 3 does). Fluency volume, long
    writing, discussion, year-long reading and topic threads across texts belong to units and sections, worksheets
    and the Reading Room, and are recorded there, not claimed by a tool.
23. **A section plan comes before a tool.** A tool is the end piece of instruction. No tool is built or refined until
    a section plan names its job, and the plan marks it key (the one a day is planned around; built first) or light
    (lighter, added over time, same standard of correctness). An existing spec or tool carries no weight against what
    the unit needs (rule 1). A teacher judges open work; a tool gives it structure and marks only what has a right
    answer. Grades 3 to 12 assume one device per student with a one-line projector path; kindergarten to grade 2 may
    assume one shared classroom device, always with a paper path; and a parent teaching one child at home is a named
    audience. Always cover the whole standard: a plan teaches and checks every part of each standard it claims, in
    both directions where the standard has two (4.NF.A.1: multiplying and dividing both numbers), even where a scope
    and sequence title names only one part (docs/courses/UNIT-PLAN.md, rule 19). All of this, with the promises it
    rests on, is in docs/FOUNDATION.md, which every spec, unit, section and review agent reads first.
# Glossary: the locked words

These are the locked words: the best known word for each thing we build, the same word inside and outside the
project (settled 2026-11-12, card 112 `q-course-level-names`, see DESK-ANSWERS "course level names"; the naming scan
is docs/reviews/naming-scan-2026-11-12.md). One row per thing we build. New prose, page copy, script messages and
Desk cards use the word in the first column. A code name (file, URL, column, id) may lag behind its word; where it
does, the row says so and why, and the staged plan in the naming scan says when it moves.

Two rules sit above the tables:

- **"Curio Class" in full**, always. `Curio.*` (the kit's code namespace, `kit/curio.js`) is code, not a name.
- **The build steps keep their names:** Idea, Spec, Build, Self-check, Refine, Fix, Exit check, Art and voice,
  Finish check, Sign-off, Live (docs/agents/decisions/BASELINE.md). Because "Exit check" is a step, nothing a
  student sees is called an exit check.

The mathematical senses of unit (unit fraction, square unit, units of measure) are not course words; they keep
their ordinary meaning.

## Older words, and where they stay

Code, ids and dated records still carry older words. Read them this way:

| Older word | Means today |
|---|---|
| stretch | unit |
| unit (the 1 to 6 day package) | section |
| stretch sheet | unit plan |
| unit plan (the old section-level plan) | section plan |
| spine, year spine | scope and sequence |
| end check | end-of-unit assessment |
| check day | assessment day |
| exit slip | exit ticket |
| Use (a day kind) | application |
| module (a topic page) | topic |
| lesson (a tool role) | teach |

**Where older names stay on purpose:** ids, item ids, folder and file names keep their old letters and words:
`s11-...` item ids, plan files `sNN.md`, section folders `uNN-N/`, `math-4-spine.md` and the
other `-spine.md` files, docs/courses/UNIT-PLAN.md (the section and unit plan template) and
docs/courses/LESSON-DAY.md, `module_id` and `data/modules.csv`, the worksheet lists' "Module" column. Each row
below names its lag.

**Records keep their words.** Dated reports in docs/reviews and docs/archive, and DESK-ANSWERS entries before its
2026-11-12 "course level names" entry, keep the words of their day: read them with the table above ("unit" there is
today's section, "stretch" is today's unit, "spine" is scope and sequence).

## The course

| Word | What it is | Do not call it | Code name and URL, if different |
|---|---|---|---|
| **course** | One grade of one subject (grade 4 math) or one high school course. What a teacher runs from day 1 to the last | class (worksheets), program, track | The frame slug (`math-grade-4`, `biology`): `docs/courses/<course>/`, `data/plans/<course>/`, `/courses/<course>/`; `data/plans/frames.csv` maps it to its catalogue id, subject and grade. Lag: grade 4 math's plan folder is still `math-4` (`legacy_plan_id`); `data/courses.csv` `course_id` (`math-k8`), the `catalog_id` column, is a subject track across grades, not a course; the worksheet code calls a course-sized list a `unit` |
| **scope and sequence** | Every day of a course's year in order, with the reason for the order, the standards and the pacing (PRODUCTION-PATH step 3, brief docs/pipeline/briefs/sequence-write.md) | spine, year spine, pacing guide (a pacing guide is the calendar only), map | `docs/courses/<course>/scope-and-sequence.md`, day rows in `data/plans/<course>/days.csv`. Lag: older files `docs/courses/<course>-spine.md` |
| **unit** | A run of course days (5 to 42 across the courses; grade 4 math 8 to 22) on one block of standards. A readiness check, given before it opens, precedes it where it assumes an earlier grade row last used two or more grades back; an end-of-unit assessment inside its days checks what it taught, and planned response follows; a unit over 25 days also holds a mid-unit assessment inside its days. The frame and the scope and sequence set its length | stretch, module, topic, chapter | Lag for good: item ids `s11-...` and plan files `sNN.md` keep the `s`; ids are never reused (LESSON-DAY rule 8); `days.csv` `stretch` |
| **section** | A short package of days (1 to 6) inside a unit, with its own plan and opener (section 11.1 is the first section of unit 11) | unit, part (taken by "part of a standard"), topic, lesson set | Lag: folders `courses/<course>/uNN-N/`, `data/units.csv` and its `unit_id` |
